# RunGuard — website environment.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. On Vercel these go in
# Settings → Environment Variables. No values here, only names.
#
# EVERY VARIABLE BELOW IS ONE THE CODE ACTUALLY READS. That is the whole point of
# this file, and it is worth keeping true: an earlier copy listed COACH_PROVIDER,
# DEMO_MODE and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, none of which are read anywhere in src/ — the
# coach model is Ollama.
# Documenting a variable that does nothing is worse than not documenting it: it
# sends whoever is deploying to look for the feature it implies.
#
# Nothing is required for local dev. With none of these set the site runs on the
# local SQLite file with the built-in rule engine.

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "sqlite" (default) uses website/data/milemarker.db and needs nothing else.
# "supabase" fails closed if the password is missing — it never falls back.
DB_DRIVER=
# Supabase only. Either DB_PASSWORD with the defaults, or a full DATABASE_URL.
DB_PASSWORD=
# DATABASE_URL=
DB_HOST=
DB_PORT=
DB_USER=
DB_NAME=
# Override where the SQLite file lives. Rarely needed.
# DATABASE_PATH=

# NOT USED, DELIBERATELY: SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_ANON_KEY,
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY.
#
# Those three belong to supabase-js, which reaches the database over HTTP
# through PostgREST. This app does not use it. src/lib/db/driver.ts opens a
# direct Postgres connection to the pooler, so DB_PASSWORD above is the only
# Supabase credential there is — the project ref is already the default DB_USER.
#
# Do not add them "ready for later", which is how they arrive. The anon key is a
# browser credential, and what currently makes the project's public REST surface
# safe is that every table has RLS enabled with no policies — so an anon caller
# reads nothing. The service-role key bypasses RLS outright: that key in a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, a client component, or a repo hands a stranger every
# athlete's HRV, sleep and injury history, and every device_token with it.
#
# A supabase-js path is not a config change. It needs RLS policies written first.

# ── Sessions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signs the coach session cookie. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# REQUIRED once deployed. src/lib/session.ts throws rather than falling back to
# the published dev default the moment DB_DRIVER=supabase or NODE_ENV=production,
# because that default is a string anyone can read in this repository — and a
# session signed with a public secret is a session anyone can mint.
SESSION_SECRET=

# ── The public demo ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The team the "See the coach dashboard" link opens, read-only. UNSET = no demo:
# the routes 404 and the landing page hides the buttons.
#
# THE TEAM YOU POINT THIS AT IS PUBLIC. Anyone with the link reads its roster,
# its check-ins and its injuries. It must contain seeded data only — the moment a
# real athlete joins it, every visitor is reading a real minor's health record.
# No code can check that for you.
#
# Read at BUILD time by the landing page, which is statically prerendered, so
# changing it needs a redeploy rather than just a restart.
DEMO_TEAM_ID=

# Optional: which runner the athlete demo opens as. Unset picks the first on the
# demo roster alphabetically. Checked against that roster, so pointing it at an
# athlete on another team falls back rather than leaking their session.
DEMO_ATHLETE_ID=

# Separate and much older: a password-only shortcut on /login. It exists ONLY
# against local SQLite outside production (see demoPassword() in
# api/login/route.ts), so it does nothing on a deployment. DEMO_TEAM_ID above is
# the one that produces the public demo.
COACH_PASSWORD=

# ── The coach chat ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DO NOT SET EITHER OF THESE ON VERCEL. They point at an Ollama server on
# localhost, which does not exist on a serverless deployment — setting them there
# buys a timeout on every question before the fallback runs.
#
# Unset is the deployed configuration, and it is a complete one: src/lib/coach.ts
# is a no-op without OLLAMA_MODEL and every question is answered by the rule
# engine in src/lib/chat.ts, reading that athlete's own last 14 days. That engine
# is also what every failure path returns, so it is the thing that has to be good
# regardless.
#
# There is no hosted-model option wired up. No code in src/ reads
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or any other provider key — if you want one, it has to be
# built, not configured.
#
# /api/chat is reachable by every demo visitor, rate-limited to 12/min/IP.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY:
# OLLAMA_MODEL=
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=          # defaults to http://localhost:11434

# ── Misc ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Absolute site URL. Two readers now, and the second one is load-bearing:
# Open Graph image URLs (link previews break without it, nothing else does), and
# the Google OAuth redirect_uri that lib/googleAuth.ts builds. That one is taken
# from configuration rather than from the request Host header on purpose —
# deriving it from the request would let a spoofed header move where Google
# delivers the authorization code. It must byte-match the redirect registered in
# the Google console or the exchange is refused.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ── Google sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unset, /api/auth/google/* answers 503 and the "Continue with Google" buttons
# still render but land on a "not set up on this deployment" message. Nothing
# else breaks; password sign-in is untouched.
#
# TWO CLIENTS, NOT ONE, because Google issues different types. The Web client is
# confidential and gets a secret; the iOS client is public and gets none. Their
# `aud` claims differ, and the code checks each against its own — accepting
# either would let a code minted for one door be redeemed through the other.

# Web client. Authorized redirect URI must be exactly
#   <NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL>/api/auth/google/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# The one real secret in this section. A browser must never see it: it is read
# only in the server-side token exchange. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# iOS client, bundle id run.runguard.app. The app authorizes against this one
# and posts its authorization code to /api/mobile/google, which redeems it here
# as a public client — no secret, PKCE instead. The phone never handles an
# id_token, which is what lets the server skip JWKS verification honestly.
#
# The app needs the SAME value as EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID, and its
# reversed form in app.json. See app/.env.example.
GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=

# ── Outbound email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only password-reset links go through this. Unset, /api/password/forgot still
# answers exactly as it does when configured — the response cannot differ, or it
# becomes a way to ask whether an address is registered — and locally it prints
# the link to the server log instead. That dev branch is fenced on the same two
# conditions session.ts uses to decide it is deployed, so a preview or
# production instance never logs a working credential; it just sends nothing.

# resend.com → API Keys → Create. Starts with `re_`. A credential: it can send
# mail as your domain.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From address. Resend's free tier will send from onboarding@resend.dev
# without any setup, but ONLY to the address that owns the Resend account —
# which is enough to test the flow and not enough to email an athlete. Emailing
# real people needs a verified domain and then noreply@<your-domain>.
MAIL_FROM=

# ── Scheduled jobs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Bearer for the nightly snapshot cron (vercel.json → /api/cron/snapshot), which
# writes one risk_snapshots row per athlete per day. Any long random string.
#
# Unset, the route answers 503 rather than running: it walks the whole roster and
# writes to the corpus, so an unset secret must not mean an open endpoint. The
# route also refuses unless DB_DRIVER=supabase, so a preview deployment cannot
# interleave its output into the same (athlete, date) rows.
CRON_SECRET=

# Lets `npm run seed` wipe a database that holds rows it did not create. Leave
# unset; the guard exists because a reseed is not recoverable.
# SEED_FORCE=
